  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 317 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nRidging will continue across South TExas through the middle of the \nweek, promoting a persistent pattern of hot, mainly dry weather. \nTemperatures will gradually climb each day, with highs reaching the \nmid- to upper 90s across the Coastal Bend and Coastal Plains, while \nthe Brush Country and Rio GRande Plains climb into the triple \ndigits. Afternoon mixing should lower dewpoints enough to keep peak \nheat index values generally between 100-110 degF. We are also looking \nat a moderate (level 2 of 4) Heat Risk will persist across much of \nthe region today and tomorrow with more pockets of major (level 3 of \n4) Heat Risk during the afternoons Wednesday/Thursday. With this in \nmind, conversations regarding the issuance of Heat Advisory products \nfor Wednesday/Thursday may become necessary with later forecast \npackages. Rain chances will remain very low through midweek due to \nthe subsident nature of the ridge.\n\nForecast confidence decreases late in the week because of current \nTropical Depression Two. The system remains poorly organized due to \nmid-level dry air limiting deep convection, though gradual \nstrengthening into what may become Tropical Storm Bertha remains \npossible later today or tonight while wind shear remains relatively \nweak. However, increasing northeasterly shear and eventually \ninteraction with the northern Gulf Coast should limit additional \nstrengthening and may lead to gradual weakening. The depression is \nexpected to drift northwest before eventually turning west as \nstrengthening mid-level ridging over the Southern Plains becomes the \nprimary steering mechanism. While the latest forecast track has \nshifted slightly north and keeps the center near or along the \nnorthern Gulf Coast, forecast confidence in both track and intensity \nremains low.\n\nFor us here in South Texas, the majority of deterministic and \nensemble guidance continues to favor the more significant impacts \nremaining north and east of the region. However, given the \naforementioned uncertainty surrounding the system's evolution, low-\nend rain chances remain in the forecast late this week to account \nfor the possibility of that deeper tropical moisture or outer rain \nbands spread farther westward. Additionally, regardless of the \ncyclone's exact track, increasing easterly swell is expected to \narrive along the Middle Texas Coast late this week, leading to \ndeteriorating marine conditions with rougher surf, a heightened risk \nof rip currents, and an increasing potential for minor coastal \nflooding.",
